@Tutorial(time: 30) {
    @Intro(title: "Moving the player with code") {
        It's time to code! We're going to use the input actions we created earlier
        to move the character. With the axolotl model now in the scene, you'll
        see it rotate to face its movement direction.

        > Note: Xogot uses GDScript, and we'll follow the GDScript naming
        > conventions in this project:
        >
        > - Classes (nodes) use PascalCase.
        > - Variables and functions use snake\_case.
        > - Constants use ALL\_CAPS.
        >
        > See the [GDScript style guide](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript%5Fstyleguide.html#doc-gdscript-styleguide)
        > for more details, and be careful to type method names precisely when
        > connecting signals.

        @Image(source: 01.attach_script_to_player.webp)
    }
    @Section(title: "Starting the player script") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We'll start by adding an empty script to the `Player` node. We'll then
            define the class's properties: a movement speed, a fall acceleration
            representing gravity, and a velocity we'll use to move the character.

            The `target_velocity` is a
            [3D vector](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fvector3.html#class-vector3)
            combining a speed with a direction. Here, we define it as a property
            because we want to update and reuse its value across frames.

            > Note: The values are quite different from 2D code because distances are
            > in meters. While in 2D, a thousand units (pixels) may only correspond
            > to half of your screen's width, in 3D, it's a kilometer.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Long-press the `Player` node and select *Attach Script* to add a new
                script to it. In the popup, set the *Template* to *Empty* before
                tapping the *Create* button. We set it to *Empty* because we want
                to write our own code for player movement.

                > Tip: Picking *Empty* ensures the script only contains the
                > `extends` line we need. The default template would add `func
                > _ready()` and `func _process()` stubs that we'd just delete.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-00.gd, reset: true)
            }
            @Step {
                Add the following properties to the top of the script. They define
                the player's movement speed, a fall acceleration representing
                gravity, and a velocity we'll reuse across frames.

                > Note: The values are quite different from 2D code because
                > distances are in meters. While in 2D a thousand units (pixels)
                > may only correspond to half of your screen's width, in 3D it's
                > a kilometer.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-01.gd, previousFile: player-00.gd)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Coding the movement") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Let's code the movement. We start by calculating the input direction
            vector using the global `Input` object, in `_physics_process()`.

            Here, instead of `_process()`, we're going to make all calculations
            using the `_physics_process()` virtual function. It's designed
            specifically for physics-related code like moving a kinematic or rigid
            body. It updates the node using fixed time intervals.

            > See also: To learn more about the difference between `_process()` and
            > `_physics_process()`, see
            > [Idle and Physics Processing](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/idle%5Fand%5Fphysics%5Fprocessing.html#doc-idle-and-physics-processing).

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                We start by initializing a `direction` variable to `Vector3.ZERO`.
                Then, we check if the player is pressing one or more of the
                `move_*` inputs and update the vector's `x` and `z` components
                accordingly. These correspond to the ground plane's axes.

                These four conditions give us eight possibilities and eight
                possible directions.

                > Note: The XZ plane is the ground plane in 3D. We never touch
                > the Y component in this section; gravity will be applied to it
                > in a later step.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-02.gd, previousFile: player-01.gd)
            }
            In case the player presses, say, both W and D simultaneously, the
            vector will have a length of about `1.4`. But if they press a single
            key, it will have a length of `1`. We want the vector's length to be
            consistent, and not move faster diagonally.

            > Tip: Diagonal movement without normalization would be roughly
            > 41% faster than cardinal movement. Calling `normalized()` on a
            > 2-component input vector returns a unit vector, so the speed
            > stays the same in every direction.

            @Step {
                Call the vector's `normalized()` method to keep the length
                consistent. We only normalize the vector if the direction has a
                length greater than zero, which means the player is pressing a
                direction key.

                We compute the direction the `$Pivot` is looking by creating a
                [Basis](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fbasis.html#class-basis)
                that looks in the `direction` direction.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-03.gd, previousFile: player-02.gd)
            }
            Then, we update the velocity. We have to calculate the ground
            velocity and the fall speed separately. Be sure to go back one tab
            so the lines are inside the `_physics_process()` function but
            outside the condition we just wrote above.

            @Step {
                Calculate the ground velocity by multiplying the direction's
                horizontal components by `speed`.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-04.gd, previousFile: player-03.gd)
            }
            @Step {
                The `CharacterBody3D.is_on_floor()` function returns `true` if
                the body collided with the floor in this frame. For the vertical
                velocity, we subtract the fall acceleration multiplied by the
                delta time every frame. This causes our character to fall in
                every frame, as long as it is not on or colliding with the floor.

                > Note: That's why we apply gravity to the `Player` only while
                > it is in the air. Without this guard, the character would
                > keep accelerating downward and clip through the floor.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-05.gd, previousFile: player-04.gd)
            }
            The physics engine can only detect interactions with walls, the
            floor, or other bodies during a given frame if movement and
            collisions happen. We will use this property later to code the
            jump.

            @Step {
                On the last line, we call `CharacterBody3D.move_and_slide()`
                which is a powerful method of the `CharacterBody3D` class that
                allows you to move a character smoothly. If it hits a wall
                midway through a motion, the engine will try to smooth it out
                for you. It uses the _velocity_ value native to the
                [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d).

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-06.gd, previousFile: player-05.gd)
            }
            And that's all the code you need to move the character on the floor.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Testing the player's movement") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We're going to put our player in the `Main` scene to test it. To do
            so, we need to instantiate the player and then add a camera. Unlike
            in 2D, in 3D, you won't see anything if your viewport doesn't have a
            camera pointing at something.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Save your `Player` scene and open the `Main` scene. You can tap
                on the _Main_ tab at the top of the editor to do so.

                If you closed the scene before, head to the _File Pad_ and
                double-tap `main.tscn` to re-open it.

                @Image(source: 02.clicking_main_tab.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                To instantiate the `Player`, long-press the `Main` node and
                select _Instantiate Child Scene_.

                In the popup, double-tap `player.tscn`. The character should
                appear in the center of the viewport.

                @Image(source: 03.instance_child_scene.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Let's add the camera next. Like we did with our _Player_'s
                _Pivot_, we're going to create a basic rig. With the `Main` node
                selected, tap the **Create New Node** button (the "+" icon) and add a
                [Marker3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fmarker3d.html#class-marker3d).
                Name it `CameraPivot`. With `CameraPivot` selected, tap the
                **Create New Node** button again and add a
                [Camera3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcamera3d.html#class-camera3d)
                child. Your scene tree should look similar to this.

                @Image(source: 04.scene_tree_with_camera.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Notice the _Preview_ checkbox that appears in the top-left of
                the 3D view when you have the _Camera_ selected. You can tap it
                to preview the in-game camera projection.

                @Image(source: 05.camera_preview_checkbox.webp)
            }
            We're going to use the _Pivot_ to rotate the camera as if it was
            on a crane. Let's first split the 3D view to be able to freely
            navigate the scene and see what the camera sees.

            @Step {
                In the 3D editor toolbar above the viewport, tap the **View**
                menu, then choose _2 Viewports_.

                @Image(source: 12.viewport_change.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                On the bottom view, select your
                [Camera3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcamera3d.html#class-camera3d)
                and turn on camera Preview by tapping the checkbox.

                @Image(source: 06.two_viewports.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In the top view, make sure your _Camera3D_ is selected and
                move the camera about `19` units on the Z axis (drag the blue
                arrow).

                @Image(source: 07.camera_preview_checkbox.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Select the _CameraPivot_ and rotate it `-45` degrees around
                the X axis (using the red circle). You'll see the camera move
                as if it was attached to a crane.

                @Image(source: 08.camera_moved.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Run the scene by tapping the **Run Project** button in the
                toolbar. Use the on-screen controls, an external keyboard's arrow
                keys, or a gamepad joystick to move the character.

                > Tip: If you have an external keyboard attached, you can also
                > try the WASD keys in addition to the arrow keys.

                @Image(source: 09.camera_rotated.png)
            }
            We can see some empty space around the character due to the
            perspective projection. In this game, we're going to use an
            orthographic projection instead to better frame the gameplay
            area and make it easier for the player to read distances.

            @Step {
                Select the _Camera_ again and in the _Inspector_, set the
                _Projection_ to _Orthogonal_ and the _Size_ to `19`. The
                character should now look flatter and the ground should fill
                the background.

                > Note: When using an orthogonal camera in Xogot, directional
                > shadow quality is dependent on the camera's _Far_ value. The
                > higher the _Far_ value, the further away the camera will be
                > able to see. However, higher _Far_ values also decrease shadow
                > quality as the shadow rendering has to cover a greater
                > distance.
                >
                > If directional shadows look too blurry after switching to an
                > orthogonal camera, decrease the camera's _Far_ property to a
                > lower value such as `100`. Don't decrease this _Far_ property
                > too much, or objects in the distance will start disappearing.

                @Image(source: 13.camera3d_values.webp)
            }
            Test your scene and you should be able to move in all 8 directions
            and not glitch through the floor!

            @Image(source: 10.camera_perspective.png)

            Ultimately, we have both player movement and the view in place.
            Next, we will work on the monsters.
        }
    }
}
